            <!-- 8. Layer 6: Virtualization Topology in Server Licensing -->
            <section class="content-section" id="hardware-topology">
                <h2>6. Server Virtualization Topology in Server Licensing</h2>
                <p>
                    For enterprise server products (like <strong>Microsoft SQL Server</strong>, <strong>Windows Server Datacenter</strong>, and <strong>Oracle Database Enterprise Edition</strong>), license compliance is calculated not by install count, but by physical and virtual compute capacity.
                </p>

                <div class="diagram-block" style="margin: 28px 0;">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Hardware Topology Hierarchy &amp; Core Multiplier Traversal</div>
                    <div class="mermaid">
                    graph TD
                        VC["vCenter Datacenter<br/>(cmdb_ci_vcenter_datacenter)"] --> CL["ESXi Cluster<br/>(cmdb_ci_cluster)"]
                        CL --> ESX1["ESXi Host 01<br/>(cmdb_ci_esx_server)<br/>2 Sockets x 16 Cores = 32 Cores"]
                        CL --> ESX2["ESXi Host 02<br/>(cmdb_ci_esx_server)<br/>2 Sockets x 16 Cores = 32 Cores"]
                        
                        ESX1 --> VM1["Virtual Machine 01 (8 vCPUs)<br/>Hosts MS SQL Server 2019"]
                        ESX1 --> VM2["Virtual Machine 02 (4 vCPUs)<br/>Hosts Application Server"]
                        ESX2 --> VM3["Virtual Machine 03 (16 vCPUs)<br/>Hosts Oracle Database 19c"]

                        classDef default fill:#161b26,stroke:#334155,stroke-width:1px,color:#f8fafc
                        classDef cluster fill:#161b26,stroke:#8b5cf6,stroke-width:2px,color:#f8fafc
                        classDef host fill:#161b26,stroke:#3b82f6,stroke-width:2px,color:#f8fafc
                        classDef vm fill:#161b26,stroke:#10b981,stroke-width:2px,color:#f8fafc

                        class VC,CL cluster
                        class ESX1,ESX2 host
                        class VM1,VM2,VM3 vm
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-card info-card--important">
                    <div class="info-card-title">Critical Licensing Calculations Governed by Topology</div>
                    <ul class="feature-bullet-list">
                        <li><strong>Microsoft Core Minimums:</strong> Microsoft licensing rules require a minimum of 4 core licenses per virtual machine (even if configured with only 1 or 2 vCPUs). If licensed at the physical host level, each physical processor requires a minimum of 8 core licenses, and the physical host requires a minimum of 16 core licenses.</li>
                        <li><strong>Oracle Processor Core Multipliers:</strong> Oracle Database Enterprise Edition utilizes a Hardware Processor Core Factor Table (e.g. 0.5 for Intel/AMD x86-64 multithreaded cores). A physical server with 32 physical cores requires: <code>32 cores * 0.5 = 16 Processor Licenses</code>.</li>
                        <li><strong>Hard vs. Soft Partitioning:</strong> In VMware vSphere environments without hard CPU pinning, Oracle audit policy considers the entire ESXi cluster as running Oracle, requiring all physical cores across all hosts to be licensed unless capped by authorized cloud partitioning.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>
    
